  <h1>The Spectacle of Protest</h1>
  <p><strong>By Lloyd Griffiths</strong></p>

  <p>[Grassroots Populism in Melbourne – Caught Between Resilience and Irrelevance
Photography and writing by Lloyd Griffiths
The Melbourne Freedom Rally (MFR) is a grassroots, right-wing populist collective. Like many populist groups across the West, it champions ideas of anti-immigration, nationalism, free speech, and a self-styled "common-sense" approach to politics.
At its height in 2021, the MFR drew thousands onto the streets. Initially sparked by opposition to COVID-19 lockdowns and vaccine mandates, the movement quickly became a lightning rod for broader anti-establishment sentiment. In its early days, the MFR was seen by many as a single-issue protest group. It provided a space where concerned citizens from across the political spectrum gathered to demand an end to COVID-19 restrictions. The crowd was diverse, unified less by ideology than by shared frustration with government mandates.
Protests organised by the group frequently ended in clashes with police. On 18th September 2021, one rally resulted in 235 arrests, with police reporting that stones, bottles, and other objects had been thrown at officers, leaving ten injured and six hospitalised. Around the same time, Harrison Mclean, the figure behind the Melbourne Freedom Rally, told his 13,000-strong Telegram group that he had been charged with incitement, a claim later confirmed by Victoria Police.
Since then, the size and energy of the movement have sharply declined. While the rallies once captured national attention during Melbourne’s long lockdowns, the events of 2025, like the recent Flagstaff Gardens gathering, suggest a movement now operating on the fringes of public life. This article explores the current state of the MFR and how it broadly mirrors the shape of populism in Australia on a larger scale.
Harrison Mclean 
Harrison Mclean, better known online by his pseudonym "Dominic D," was the founder of the MFR and the driving force behind its early protests. He would often be seen at rallies, megaphone in hand, calling for an end to what he described as a corrupt, freedom-hating establishment. Publicly, Mclean presented himself as a champion of civil liberties. However, investigations have revealed deeper ties to far-right networks and extremist ideologies.
A Guardian investigation found that Mclean had engaged with several far-right and white supremacist groups online, including a Telegram channel used by the Proud Boys, a worldwide far-right organisation designated as a terrorist entity by the Canadian government. Mclean has confirmed his membership and admitted maintaining ties with the group, inviting members to participate in the anti-lockdown protests of 2021.
Beyond these associations, Mclean has also expressed antisemitic views in private online forums. Using his pseudonym, he outlined strategies for gradually introducing his followers to radical political ideas, advising caution when exposing "normie" newcomers to antisemitic conspiracy theories. In one chat group, he wrote that while he shared "many of the concerns about the present role of the Jews," his followers were "not ready for the JQ yet," using the common white supremacist shorthand for the "Jewish Question." Mclean also offered advice on creating antisemitic memes and discussed ways to radicalise newcomers subtly without alienating them.
Publicly, the MFR maintained its broad anti-lockdown, anti-government messaging. Behind the scenes, however, it increasingly served as an entry point into a wider world of conspiracy theories and extremist ideologies that flourished during the pandemic.
Mclean has also commented on the presence of Melbourne’s neo-Nazi group, the National Socialist Network, within MFR spaces. Aware of the more mainstream participants drawn to his rallies, he advised that extremists should "moderate their message" if they wished to remain involved. The MFR was less a tightly controlled movement than a stepping stone, offering newcomers to political activism gradual exposure to more radical views.
Today, the MFR continues to present itself as a community-driven hub for those disillusioned with mainstream politics. It hosts regular barbecues and informal gatherings in public parks, providing a sense of belonging to its members. Yet the cost of participation, and the potential for deeper radicalisation, remains an open and largely unspoken question.
 
Before the Speakers
I invited myself to the most recent rally after gaining access to the MFR Telegram page. After scrolling through multiple memes with multiculturalism as the punchline and recent media stunts pulled by Mclean, I found a date and time along with an itinerary of 9 guest speakers.
The latest Melbourne Freedom Rally was held in Flagstaff Gardens, an area heavily populated by immigrants, particularly international students. The choice of location added an unmistakable layer of irony. In a gathering framed around defending ‘traditional Australia’, the backdrop of multicultural Melbourne was a case study of what the MFR stood against. Whether intentional or not, the setting seemed symbolic of the broader tensions the group seeks to highlight.
The speeches were scheduled to begin at midday. Arriving early, I scanned the park for signs of the chaotic crowds once associated with the MFR during the height of the lockdowns. I expected megaphones, chanting, and confrontations with police. None of that materialised. Instead, two men were setting up a pair of speakers under a tree, one of them Harrison Mclean.
Arriving fifteen minutes ahead of time, I had hoped to observe a crowd and speak to participants. Instead, Guns N’ Roses blared from the speakers as a handful of people slowly trickled in. It was clear that the MFR was no longer what it had once been. The momentum built during the lockdown era had not carried through to 2025. About an hour after the scheduled start, Mclean finally took to the mic to open the event.

 
Speeches and Rally
By this point, a crowd of no more than 40 had arrived, many holding GoPros and selfie sticks, live-streaming to online audiences. Symbols and slogans adorned their clothing, from biker gang logos on hoodies to "Don't Tread on Me" flags draped across shoulders and Orwellian slogans on t-shirts.
Only five of the nine scheduled speakers turned up. Despite representing different parties, their speeches echoed the same themes: heavy populist rhetoric, conspiracy theories and anti-establishment sentiment. Among the more notable speakers were Morgan C. Jonas, leader of the Freedom Party, Dr. Chris Neil, Senate candidate for the People’s First Party, and Jordan Dittloff, Senate candidate for the Libertarian Party. Much of the discourse revisited familiar territory, including opposition to COVID vaccines, distrust of lockdown protocols, and a belief in elite control by the so-called Labor and Liberal "Uni-Party" or global organisations like the World Health Organization. Terms like "managed decline" were thrown about, with speakers vowing to expose corruption if elected.
The notion of a “Uni-Party” seemed to be a key talking point among the speakers, understandably so, in the lead-up to next week’s federal election. Despite representing different parties, they consistently framed themselves as a unified opposition to the dominant political establishment. Calls for cross-party cooperation highlighted a growing recognition among populists that fragmented efforts have limited their political impact to date.
The crowd’s reaction was mixed. True believers cheered and waved flags, while those out for a weekend stroll through the park looked on, puzzled. A few hecklers shouted from the sidelines, only to be moved on by listeners pausing between crushing beer cans and reaching for another.
After an hour of speeches, the group, now reduced to about 10 or 15, set off through Melbourne’s CBD, marching to the sound of dad rock and chants of "Australia for the Australians." However, despite Mclean’s claim that the rally route had been pre-approved, the march was abruptly stopped by Victorian Police. Not because of any disruption by the MFR, but because a larger, unrelated protest by what Mclean labelled "Radical Trans Rights Activists" was unfolding nearby. With no clear path forward, the group was left waiting. The fight against the elite would have to be postponed.
The contrast with earlier rallies was stark. Those who once clashed with police in the name of ‘freedom’ had largely moved on. Mclean insists this marks a new era for the MFR, one focused on cultivating a tight-knit, dedicated community rather than mass mobilisation. 
 
A Mirror of Populism in Australia
The story of the MFR reflects the broader trajectory of populism in Australia. Fragmented, regional, and struggling to sustain momentum, Australian populism continues to operate on the fringes of political life. Despite the far-right’s loud presence in online spaces, its real-world reach remains limited.
At the rally, speakers framed themselves as a cross-party opposition to the political establishment, recognising the challenges posed by a splintered movement. Yet, in practice, Australia’s populists remain divided across numerous small parties and independent groups. Unlike countries in Europe, where right-wing populist movements are both challenging and entering government, Australian populism has yet to break through nationally.
Structural factors play a major role. Compulsory voting, preferential ballots, and a relatively strong centrist tradition all make it difficult for fringe movements to consolidate significant political power. Instead of forming a major new party capable of contesting government, Australian populists have splintered into minor parties such as One Nation, the United Australia Party, the Libertarians, and the Freedom Party. Their influence is felt more in the way they pressure major parties to adjust or harden positions on issues like immigration, national sovereignty, and public health.
Yet, as the Freedom Rally shows, energy and numbers are hard to sustain without a galvanising crisis like COVID-19. Without mandates or lockdowns to oppose, much of the fire that once drove the MFR has burned out, leaving only a hardened core of loyalists. Populism in Australia is far from dead, but it remains a movement caught between resilience and irrelevance.
Despite its limited reach compared to other Western democracies, populism continues to shape parts of Australia’s political debate, slowly shifting the Overton Window to the right. Recognising the cost of fragmentation, figures like Harrison Mclean and Morgan Jonas have begun pushing for greater unity. Jonas recently announced on Facebook his intention to avoid competing with aligned parties in the upcoming federal election and hinted at possible mergers in the future.
The force of populism may yet find firmer footing on Australian shores. For now, though, it remains a scattered and struggling movement. Even the MFR’s own website reflects the current state of affairs, having recently been hacked by what appears to be an Indonesian online poker company advertising gambling services to Mclean’s dwindling audience. 

